WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.167
Look up the name. The browser or operating system asks a resolver for the hostname. Existing local or resolver caches may already contain an answer.

2
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:23.250
Query authority if needed. A resolver without a usable cached answer follows DNS resolution to the authoritative records. Record type and delegation must be configured correctly.

3
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:34.167
Use a cached answer. A client may connect to the old endpoint while its answer is still valid. Changing the authoritative record does not instantly erase that cached answer.

4
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:45.750
Fetch the new answer. Later lookups can obtain the new destination. Clients and resolvers change at different times, so old and new endpoints may both receive traffic.

5
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:57.000
Verify beyond DNS. The new address also needs the correct certificate, host routing and healthy application. Successful resolution alone does not prove the website works.

6
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:10.083
Plan safe overlap. Keep a rollback path and an appropriate overlap interval. Lower TTL in advance when useful; do not expect a last-second TTL edit to end existing connections.
